<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Terqa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tell Ashara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Terqa was an ancient city on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, at the site of Tell Ashara. A centre of the cult of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a>, it belonged to the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> and after Mari's fall became the capital of the Kingdom of Hana in the 2nd millennium BCE. Excavations found a house with a jar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a>, evidence of very long-distance trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/terqa.jpg" alt="Terqa" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mission franco-syrienne de tell Masaîkh. · CC BY-SA 3.0 fr · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Terqa_-_Ashara_-_Syrie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Terqa was an ancient city at the site of Tell Ashara on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in the Deir ez-Zor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, about 80 km from the modern border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and 64 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>. By Neo-Assyrian times its name had become Sirqu. It stood near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a>, a trade hub on the Euphrates and Khabur rivers, with Mari to the south and Tuttul (Tell Bi'a) to the north. It ruled a larger hinterland, but because the valley could hold only one main political centre it was always second to Mari. Its principal god was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Little is known of its early history, though it was already sizable in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a>. In the Early Bronze Age Ebla and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> competed for hegemony on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and Terqa was contested between them; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> later took control. It was an urban centre with a massive defensive wall, yet a provincial city under Mari. Yahdun-Lim of Mari commemorated building the walls of Mari and Terqa in a year-name, and the city then came under Shamshi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a> of the Amorite Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia. Zimri-Lim named a year for offering a great throne to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a> of Terqa, and it was governed for him by Kibri-Dagan.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> attacked his former ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> and destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, power in the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> shifted to Terqa, which became the leading city of the kingdom of Hana after the decline of Babylon. About the middle of the 15th century BCE it came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> kingdom, and the kings Sinia and Qiš-Addu ruled in that time. Later Terqa fell within the sphere of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassite-dynasty">Kassite dynasty</a> of Babylon and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a>; a stele of Tukulti-Ninurta II was found nearby.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Ernst Herzfeld briefly excavated the site in 1910, and François Thureau-Dangin and P. Dhorme worked there for five days in 1923. A team from the International Institute for Mesopotamian Area Studies, led by Giorgio Buccellati and Marilyn Kelly-Buccellati, excavated for ten seasons from 1974 to 1986, and a French team under Olivier Rouault continued until about 2010. Two thirds of the remains lie under the modern town of Ashara, and about half the original site has been eroded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>.</p><p>The finds include a city wall of three concentric masonry walls, built about 2900, 2800 and 2700 BCE and in use until at least 2000 BCE; a temple of the healing goddess Ninkarrak, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> of agate, carnelian and lapis lazuli and bronze figurines of dogs, her sacred animals; and the House of Puzurum, which held an archive of contracts from the time of the kingdom of Hana. The Deir ez-Zor Museum keeps 550 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets from Terqa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Terqa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the last decades of the archive Ebla turned the tables: in alliance with Nagar and Kish it defeated Mari in a battle apparently fought near <b>Terqa</b> on the Euphrates, an event recorded in the tablets.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ebla">Ebla · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Zimri-Lim claimed to have built an ice house at <b>Terqa</b>, a feat he said no earlier king had achieved, to keep ice for cooling drinks at court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mari">Mari · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Babylonian Empire destroyed Mari c. 1760 BCE, local rulers at <b>Terqa</b>, some 70 kilometres upstream and long the seat of the god Dagan, created the kingdom of Hana.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hana">Kingdom of Hana</a></li></ul></section>
